/* PsychLogic — Clinical Mock 9 (Clinical Psychology in Practice) question bank. */
window.MOCK_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 1, "question": "Over the past year, Ramesh’s drinking has caused liver damage and fights at home, but he shows no loss of control, tolerance or withdrawal. In ICD-11 this is:", "options": ["Harmful pattern of use of alcohol", "Alcohol dependence", "Episode of harmful use of alcohol", "Hazardous alcohol use"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 ‘harmful pattern of use’ is repeated use that has damaged physical or mental health (or harmed others) over at least 12 months (or 1 month if continuous), without dependence. It corresponds to ICD-10 harmful use (F10.1).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Dependence needs impaired control and often tolerance or withdrawal.</li><li>‘Episode of harmful use’ is a single episode, not a year-long pattern.</li><li>‘Hazardous use’ means increased risk, before actual harm has occurred.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-11 also has ‘episode of harmful use’ for a single damaging episode.</li><li>ICD-11 alcohol disorders are coded 6C40.</li><li>‘Hazardous alcohol use’ is coded outside the mental disorders chapter.</li><li>In DSM-5, stuttering (childhood-onset fluency disorder) is classified under:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — JANAS. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 2, "question": "In DSM-5, stuttering (childhood-onset fluency disorder) is classified under:", "options": ["Trauma- and stressor-related disorders", "Communication disorders (neurodevelopmental)", "Anxiety disorders", "Motor disorders — tic disorders"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 places childhood-onset fluency disorder with the communication disorders in the neurodevelopmental chapter, alongside language disorder, speech sound disorder and social (pragmatic) communication disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Anxiety may worsen stuttering, but it is not an anxiety disorder.</li><li>Tics are sudden movements or sounds, not fluency problems.</li><li>It is not caused by trauma by definition.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Adult-onset fluency problems are coded differently.</li><li>Speech-language pathologists treat fluency disorders.</li><li>Stuttering is more common in boys.</li><li>Collateral information from family members is especially important when assessing a patient with:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — RINPAS 2024, JEMAS 2024, JEMAS 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 3, "question": "Collateral information from family members is especially important when assessing a patient with:", "options": ["A specific phobia of dogs", "Adjustment problems after moving house", "Poor insight, such as in psychosis or dementia", "Mild exam anxiety"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> When patients lack insight or have memory problems, informants give a more reliable picture of onset, course and behaviour.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A person with mild anxiety usually reports well.</li><li>Phobias are usually well described by the person.</li><li>Adjustment problems are usually reported reliably.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Informant reliability should be recorded in the case history.</li><li>In child assessment, parents and teachers are key informants.</li><li>Confidentiality limits must be explained before contacting informants.</li><li>Two sisters inherit the same genetic risk for depression. Only the one who faces job loss and a divorce becomes depressed. This is best explained by the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — RML 2024. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 4, "question": "Two sisters inherit the same genetic risk for depression. Only the one who faces job loss and a divorce becomes depressed. This is best explained by the:", "options": ["Medical model of mental illness", "Psychodynamic theory of repression", "Labelling theory of mental illness", "Diathesis–stress model"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The diathesis (vulnerability — genetic, biological or psychological) and stress interact: the greater the vulnerability, the less stress is needed to trigger the disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Labelling theory explains how labels shape behaviour, not onset after stress.</li><li>The medical model alone would not explain why only one sister fell ill.</li><li>Repression is a psychodynamic idea, not the explanation here.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IMHANS 2025 asked how the diathesis–stress model explains schizophrenia.</li><li>Zubin and Spring’s vulnerability–stress model (1977) applied it to schizophrenia.</li><li>Meehl linked schizotaxia (diathesis) with stress.</li><li>In DSM-5, PTSD in children aged 6 years or younger may show re-experiencing mainly as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — IMHANS 2025, GBU 2025. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 5, "question": "In DSM-5, PTSD in children aged 6 years or younger may show re-experiencing mainly as:", "options": ["Psychotic hallucinations about the event", "Detailed verbal descriptions of flashbacks", "Repetitive play that re-enacts the trauma", "Loss of identity"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 has a separate PTSD criteria set for children 6 and younger. Intrusive memories may appear as play re-enacting the event, and frightening dreams may have no clear content.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Young children often cannot describe flashbacks in words.</li><li>PTSD is not a psychotic disorder.</li><li>Loss of identity is not a PTSD feature.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The preschool criteria need fewer symptoms.</li><li>Trauma-focused CBT is first-line for children.</li><li>Parents are closely involved in treatment.</li><li>All of the following ICD-10 codes are correctly matched, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, IHBAS 2024, GMCH 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 6, "question": "All of the following ICD-10 codes are correctly matched, EXCEPT:", "options": ["F31 – Bipolar affective disorder", "F25 – Schizoaffective disorders", "F32 – Depressive episode", "F22 – Acute and transient psychotic disorders"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> F22 is persistent delusional disorders. Acute and transient psychotic disorders are F23.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F32 – depressive episode is correct.</li><li>F31 – bipolar affective disorder is correct.</li><li>F25 – schizoaffective disorders is correct.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>J&amp;K 2023 asked the code for unspecified psychosis (F29).</li><li>JEMAS 2024 asked the code for schizophrenia (F20), with F22 as a distractor.</li><li>F24 – induced delusional disorder (folie à deux).</li><li>The Comprehensive System for scoring and interpreting the Rorschach was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 7, "question": "The Comprehensive System for scoring and interpreting the Rorschach was developed by:", "options": ["Samuel Beck", "John Exner", "Bruno Klopfer", "David Rapaport"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> John Exner (1974 onwards) combined earlier Rorschach systems into the Comprehensive System, with standard administration, scoring and norms.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer created two of the earlier competing systems.</li><li>David Rapaport developed a psychoanalytic scoring approach.</li><li>Exner’s system combined their best features.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>R-PAS (Rorschach Performance Assessment System, 2011) is the newer system.</li><li>The Rorschach has 10 inkblot cards.</li><li>Determinants include form, colour, shading and movement.</li><li>An accident victim loses his right hand and, as a result, becomes unemployed. This situation is best explained by the term:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 8, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · GMCH 2023</span>An accident victim loses his right hand and, as a result, becomes unemployed. This situation is best explained by the term:", "options": ["Handicap", "Disability", "Impairment", "Illness"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In WHO’s ICIDH (1980): impairment = loss of a body part or function (losing the hand); disability = restriction in doing activities (cannot write or lift); handicap = the social disadvantage that follows (losing his job).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Disability is the restriction in activity, not the social disadvantage.</li><li>Impairment is the loss of the hand itself.</li><li>Illness refers to disease, not to this outcome.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICIDH (1980) was replaced by the ICF (2001).</li><li>ICF terms: impairments, activity limitations, participation restrictions.</li><li>‘Handicap’ is now an outdated term.</li><li>In Lazarus’s transactional model of stress, secondary appraisal asks:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 9, "question": "In Lazarus’s transactional model of stress, secondary appraisal asks:", "options": ["‘How did my parents deal with stress?’", "‘Is this situation a threat to me?’", "‘Do I have the resources to cope with this?’", "‘What is my blood pressure right now?’"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Primary appraisal judges whether a situation is irrelevant, positive or stressful (harm, threat, challenge); secondary appraisal judges the resources and options for coping.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Judging whether something is a threat is primary appraisal.</li><li>Family history is not part of appraisal.</li><li>Physiological measures are not appraisal.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Lazarus and Folkman, Stress, Appraisal and Coping (1984).</li><li>Reappraisal updates judgements as the situation changes.</li><li>Stress arises when demands exceed perceived resources.</li><li>Instead of subtypes such as paranoid or hebephrenic, ICD-11 describes schizophrenia using:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2024, RML 2022, JANAS. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 10, "question": "Instead of subtypes such as paranoid or hebephrenic, ICD-11 describes schizophrenia using:", "options": ["Symptom specifiers such as positive, negative, manic and cognitive", "The same five subtypes that are used in DSM-5", "Categories based only on the age at which illness began", "Severity levels 1 to 3 based on how much support is needed"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 dropped the ICD-10 subtypes (paranoid, hebephrenic, catatonic, undifferentiated, residual, simple) and instead rates current symptom domains, plus course specifiers (first episode, multiple episodes, continuous).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Support-level severity is DSM-5’s approach for autism.</li><li>DSM-5 also dropped subtypes.</li><li>Age of onset is not how ICD-11 describes schizophrenia.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IHBAS 2025 asked the ICD-10 duration for schizophrenia (1 month).</li><li>ICD-11 code for schizophrenia: 6A20.</li><li>Simple schizophrenia (F20.6) existed in ICD-10 but not in DSM.</li><li>Which of the following is NOT a specification of the POCSO Act, 2012?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, M.Phil Entrance 2021, Gurugram. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 11, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · RINPAS 2024</span>Which of the following is NOT a specification of the POCSO Act, 2012?", "options": ["Recording the statement of the child at the residence of the child or at the place of his choice", "Police officer to not be in uniform while recording the statement of the child", "No aggressive questioning or character assassination of the child", "The child can be detained in the police station in the night"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Mental health laws & RCI", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> POCSO is child-friendly: the statement is recorded at the child’s home or chosen place, by an officer not in uniform, with no aggressive questioning. The Act clearly says that no child shall be detained in the police station at night for any reason.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Recording at the child’s home or chosen place is a POCSO provision.</li><li>The officer must not be in uniform — a POCSO provision.</li><li>No aggressive questioning or character assassination — a POCSO provision.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>POCSO defines a child as anyone below 18 years.</li><li>Section 19 makes reporting of suspected abuse mandatory; failing to report is punishable (Section 21).</li><li>Special Courts must record the child’s evidence within 30 days where possible.</li><li>🏛 Which exams ask this: Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</li><li>Five weeks after her husband died, Meera has had more than two weeks of depressed mood, feelings of worthlessness, poor sleep and thoughts that life is not worth living. Under DSM-5:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 12, "question": "Five weeks after her husband died, Meera has had more than two weeks of depressed mood, feelings of worthlessness, poor sleep and thoughts that life is not worth living. Under DSM-5:", "options": ["Only adjustment disorder can be diagnosed, as there is a clear stressor", "MDD can be diagnosed if full criteria are met, as the bereavement exclusion was removed", "Only prolonged grief disorder can be diagnosed at this stage", "MDD cannot be diagnosed until 2 months have passed since the death"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-IV excluded major depression in the first 2 months after a loss (the ‘bereavement exclusion’). DSM-5 removed this exclusion, so MDD can be diagnosed when full criteria are met, using clinical judgement to tell it apart from normal grief.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The 2-month rule was the DSM-IV bereavement exclusion, removed in DSM-5.</li><li>Prolonged grief disorder needs at least 12 months after the death in adults (DSM-5-TR).</li><li>Adjustment disorder is used when criteria for another disorder are not met.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Worthlessness and suicidal thoughts point more to depression than to normal grief.</li><li>In grief, painful feelings come in waves linked to reminders of the loss.</li><li>ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder needs at least 6 months.</li><li>The Indian Association of Clinical Psychologists (IACP) was founded in:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — RINPAS 2024, RML 2022, JEMAS 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 13, "question": "The Indian Association of Clinical Psychologists (IACP) was founded in:", "options": ["1968", "1925", "1955", "1993"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The IACP, the main professional body for clinical psychologists in India, was founded in 1968 and publishes the Indian Journal of Clinical Psychology.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>1925 – the Indian Psychological Association was formed.</li><li>1955 – the first clinical psychology training course began (AIIMH).</li><li>1993 – RCI became a statutory body.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RINPAS 2019 asked when the Indian Psychological Association was formed.</li><li>The National Academy of Psychology (India) was founded in 1989.</li><li>IACP holds an annual national conference (NACIACP).</li><li>Compared with DSM-5, the ICD:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 14, "question": "Compared with DSM-5, the ICD:", "options": ["Covers only mental and behavioural disorders, like the DSM", "Is published by a professional body in the United States", "Covers all health conditions and is used for world health statistics", "Is used officially only in the United States of America"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The ICD is the WHO’s global classification of all diseases, used for mortality and morbidity statistics, health planning and insurance. Its mental disorders chapter is one part of it. The DSM covers only mental disorders.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>That describes the DSM.</li><li>The DSM is published by the American Psychiatric Association; the ICD by the WHO.</li><li>The ICD is used by WHO member states worldwide.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-11 is free to use; the DSM is sold commercially.</li><li>In the USA, DSM diagnoses are billed with ICD-10-CM codes.</li><li>ICD diagnostic guidelines are usually more flexible than DSM criteria.</li><li>A psychologist writes in a report: ‘You have a great need for others to like you, yet you are often critical of yourself.’ Almost anyone would agree. Such vague statements exploit the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, JEMAS 2024, NFSU 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 15, "question": "A psychologist writes in a report: ‘You have a great need for others to like you, yet you are often critical of yourself.’ Almost anyone would agree. Such vague statements exploit the:", "options": ["Hawthorne effect", "Barnum (Forer) effect", "Pygmalion effect", "Halo effect"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Barnum or Forer effect is the tendency to accept vague, general statements as accurate personal descriptions. Good assessment reports must be specific to the person.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The halo effect is one positive trait colouring judgement of others.</li><li>The Hawthorne effect is behaviour changing because people know they are observed.</li><li>The Pygmalion effect is others’ expectations shaping performance.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Bertram Forer demonstrated it in 1949 with students.</li><li>Meehl named it after P.T. Barnum.</li><li>It explains the appeal of horoscopes and some personality quizzes.</li><li>All of the following are new diagnostic categories in ICD-11 that were not in ICD-10, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — Gurugram. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 16, "question": "All of the following are new diagnostic categories in ICD-11 that were not in ICD-10, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Compulsive sexual behaviour disorder", "Prolonged grief disorder", "Body integrity dysphoria", "Schizotypal disorder"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Schizotypal disorder already existed in ICD-10 (F21), placed with schizophrenia. Body integrity dysphoria, compulsive sexual behaviour disorder and prolonged grief disorder are new in ICD-11.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Body integrity dysphoria is new in ICD-11.</li><li>Compulsive sexual behaviour disorder is new in ICD-11.</li><li>Prolonged grief disorder is new in ICD-11.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Other ICD-11 additions: complex PTSD, gaming disorder, hoarding disorder, binge eating disorder, bipolar type II.</li><li>ICD-11 keeps schizotypal disorder with the primary psychotic disorders (6A22).</li><li>DSM-5 lists schizotypal personality disorder in both the personality and schizophrenia-spectrum chapters.</li><li>During an MSE, an elderly patient knows his own name but not the date, the day, or that he is in a hospital. His attention fluctuates. This pattern points first to:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — JEPAS 2020. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 17, "question": "During an MSE, an elderly patient knows his own name but not the date, the day, or that he is in a hospital. His attention fluctuates. This pattern points first to:", "options": ["Dissociative amnesia for personal identity", "Normal ageing-related forgetfulness", "Disorientation in time and place, suggesting an organic cause such as delirium", "A primary psychotic disorder such as schizophrenia"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Orientation to time is lost first, then place, then person. Disorientation with fluctuating attention strongly suggests delirium or another organic state, not a functional psychosis.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>In schizophrenia, orientation is usually intact.</li><li>In dissociative amnesia, identity is lost, while time and place are often preserved.</li><li>Normal ageing does not cause disorientation.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IHBAS 2025 asked the key feature separating dementia from delirium.</li><li>Orientation is lost in the order time → place → person.</li><li>Delirium is a medical emergency.</li><li>All of the following were introduced as new diagnoses in DSM-5, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 18, "question": "All of the following were introduced as new diagnoses in DSM-5, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Excoriation (skin-picking) disorder", "Body dysmorphic disorder", "Social (pragmatic) communication disorder", "Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Body dysmorphic disorder already existed in DSM-IV (as a somatoform disorder) and was only moved to the OC-related chapter. DMDD, social (pragmatic) communication disorder and excoriation disorder were new in DSM-5.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DMDD was new in DSM-5.</li><li>Social (pragmatic) communication disorder was new in DSM-5.</li><li>Excoriation disorder was new in DSM-5.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Other DSM-5 additions: hoarding disorder, binge-eating disorder (promoted), PMDD (promoted), mild neurocognitive disorder.</li><li>DSM-5 removed the multiaxial system and the schizophrenia subtypes.</li><li>RINPAS 2024 asked which behaviours are not included in DSM-5 ASD.</li><li>The ‘bell-and-pad’ (enuresis alarm) method for bedwetting is based mainly on:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IMHANS 2025, IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 19, "question": "The ‘bell-and-pad’ (enuresis alarm) method for bedwetting is based mainly on:", "options": ["Psychoanalytic interpretation", "Operant extinction", "Cognitive restructuring", "Classical conditioning"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Developed by Mowrer and Mowrer (1938), the alarm wakes the child as wetting begins; bladder fullness becomes linked with waking (classical conditioning, with avoidance learning).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Extinction removes reinforcement; that is not how the alarm works.</li><li>No thoughts are changed.</li><li>No unconscious material is interpreted.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The alarm is the most effective long-term treatment for nocturnal enuresis.</li><li>Desmopressin is a medication option.</li><li>Overlearning (extra fluids once dry) reduces relapse.</li><li>The first formal training course in clinical psychology in India (the Diploma in Medical and Social Psychology) began in 1955 at:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 20, "question": "The first formal training course in clinical psychology in India (the Diploma in Medical and Social Psychology) began in 1955 at:", "options": ["All India Institute of Mental Health, Bangalore", "Central Institute of Psychiatry, Ranchi", "Institute of Human Behaviour and Allied Sciences, Delhi", "University of Calcutta"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The All India Institute of Mental Health (AIIMH), set up in Bangalore in 1954 and later merged into NIMHANS, started the DM&amp;SP course in 1955.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>CIP Ranchi (founded 1918) started its clinical psychology course later.</li><li>Calcutta had the first psychology department (1916), not this course.</li><li>IHBAS was set up much later (1993).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>AIIMH became part of NIMHANS in 1974.</li><li>The DM&amp;SP later became the M.Phil in Medical and Social Psychology/Clinical Psychology.</li><li>CIP Ranchi began as the European Lunatic Asylum in 1918.</li><li>Ader and Cohen’s 1975 study, which helped found psychoneuroimmunology, showed that:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — NIMHANS 2023 (memory-based). We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 21, "question": "Ader and Cohen’s 1975 study, which helped found psychoneuroimmunology, showed that:", "options": ["The immune system is completely independent of the brain", "Stress always improves immune function", "Placebos have no effect on the body", "Suppression of the immune system can be classically conditioned"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Rats given saccharin water with an immune-suppressing drug later showed immune suppression to saccharin alone — the immune response had been classically conditioned.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Chronic stress usually weakens immunity.</li><li>The study showed close brain–immune links.</li><li>It supports, rather than rejects, mind–body effects such as placebo.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Robert Ader coined the term ‘psychoneuroimmunology’.</li><li>Kiecolt-Glaser showed that exam stress slows wound healing.</li><li>Cortisol from the HPA axis suppresses immune activity.</li><li>The ICD-10 duration criterion for schizophrenia requires symptoms to persist for at least:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — IMHANS 2025. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 22, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IHBAS 2025</span>The ICD-10 duration criterion for schizophrenia requires symptoms to persist for at least:", "options": ["6 months", "2 weeks", "3 months", "1 month"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-10 needs clear schizophrenic symptoms for most of the time during at least 1 month. DSM-5 needs a total of 6 months (with at least 1 month of active symptoms). ICD-11 keeps the 1-month rule.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>6 months is the DSM-5 total duration.</li><li>2 weeks is not the ICD-10 schizophrenia rule.</li><li>3 months is not a criterion in either system.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>In ICD-10, symptoms for less than 1 month suggest acute schizophrenia-like psychotic disorder (F23.2).</li><li>JEMAS 2024 asked for the ICD-10 code of schizophrenia (F20).</li><li>This ICD–DSM difference is a favourite exam trap.</li><li>The concept of ‘daily hassles’ as a source of stress, measured by the Hassles Scale, was introduced by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 23, "question": "The concept of ‘daily hassles’ as a source of stress, measured by the Hassles Scale, was introduced by:", "options": ["Hans Selye", "Kanner, Coyne, Schaefer and Lazarus", "Holmes and Rahe", "Friedman and Rosenman"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Kanner, Coyne, Schaefer and Lazarus (1981) showed that minor everyday irritations predict health and mood better than major life events.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Holmes and Rahe measured major life events.</li><li>Selye described the General Adaptation Syndrome.</li><li>Friedman and Rosenman described Type A behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Uplifts Scale measures small positive events.</li><li>Hassles add up over time.</li><li>IHBAS 2025 asked what the Holmes–Rahe scale focuses on.</li><li>Under DSM-5-TR, prolonged grief disorder in children and adolescents can be diagnosed when at least how much time has passed since the death?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — IHBAS 2025. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 24, "question": "Under DSM-5-TR, prolonged grief disorder in children and adolescents can be diagnosed when at least how much time has passed since the death?", "options": ["6 months", "12 months", "2 months", "2 years"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5-TR requires at least 12 months since the death for adults, but at least 6 months for children and adolescents. ICD-11 uses at least 6 months for all ages.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>12 months is the adult rule.</li><li>2 months was the old bereavement-exclusion window.</li><li>2 years is not a grief criterion.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Core symptom: intense yearning for, or preoccupation with, the deceased.</li><li>DSM-5-TR placed prolonged grief disorder in the trauma- and stressor-related chapter.</li><li>It was ‘persistent complex bereavement disorder’ in DSM-5 Section III.</li><li>The ‘four Ds’ often used to describe abnormality are:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 25, "question": "The ‘four Ds’ often used to describe abnormality are:", "options": ["Depression, dementia, delirium and dissociation", "Diagnosis, description, development and dynamics", "Deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger", "Delusion, disorder, disability and denial"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Abnormal behaviour is commonly described as deviant (from social norms), distressing, dysfunctional (interfering with daily life) and sometimes dangerous.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>These mix symptoms and defences, not criteria of abnormality.</li><li>These are specific conditions, not criteria.</li><li>These are not the four Ds.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Other approaches: statistical rarity, deviation from ideal mental health (Jahoda), and harmful dysfunction (Wakefield).</li><li>No single criterion is enough on its own.</li><li>GBU 2025 asked how abnormal behaviour is defined (deviation from typical patterns).</li><li>A 4-year-old girl raised in an orphanage goes off with strangers without hesitation and does not check back with her caregiver. She is overly friendly with unfamiliar adults. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — GBU 2025, NFSU 2023, J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 26, "question": "A 4-year-old girl raised in an orphanage goes off with strangers without hesitation and does not check back with her caregiver. She is overly friendly with unfamiliar adults. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:", "options": ["Disinhibited social engagement disorder", "Reactive attachment disorder", "Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder", "Separation anxiety disorder"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Disinhibited social engagement disorder follows severe social neglect and shows overly familiar behaviour with strangers and little checking back with caregivers.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Reactive attachment disorder is the opposite: inhibited, rarely seeking comfort.</li><li>ADHD involves inattention and hyperactivity, not indiscriminate friendliness.</li><li>Separation anxiety involves fear of separation.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Both RAD and DSED need a developmental age of at least 9 months.</li><li>Both are in the trauma- and stressor-related chapter.</li><li>Studies of Romanian orphans showed these patterns.</li><li>For 3 years, a 30-year-old has felt low most of the day on most days, and for the last month she has also met full criteria for a major depressive episode. Under DSM-5 the best diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 27, "question": "For 3 years, a 30-year-old has felt low most of the day on most days, and for the last month she has also met full criteria for a major depressive episode. Under DSM-5 the best diagnosis is:", "options": ["Major depressive disorder, single episode only", "‘Double depression’, a separate DSM-5 diagnosis", "Persistent depressive disorder, with major depressive episodes", "Cyclothymic disorder with depressive features"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 merged dysthymia and chronic major depression into persistent depressive disorder (2 years in adults). If major depressive episodes occur during it, this is recorded with a specifier rather than a separate ‘double depression’ diagnosis.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>MDD alone misses the 3-year chronic course.</li><li>‘Double depression’ is a clinical term, not a DSM-5 diagnosis.</li><li>Cyclothymia needs hypomanic symptoms too.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Persistent depressive disorder needs 2 years in adults and 1 year in children.</li><li>Symptom-free gaps cannot last more than 2 months.</li><li>ICD-11 keeps ‘dysthymic disorder’ (6A72).</li><li>A semi-structured diagnostic interview widely used to assess mood, anxiety and other disorders in children and adolescents is the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — IHBAS 2025. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 28, "question": "A semi-structured diagnostic interview widely used to assess mood, anxiety and other disorders in children and adolescents is the:", "options": ["Conners Rating Scales", "K-SADS", "Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales", "Child Behavior Checklist (CBCL)"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Kiddie Schedule for Affective Disorders and Schizophrenia (K-SADS) is a semi-structured interview with the child and parents that gives DSM-based diagnoses.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The CBCL is a parent-report rating checklist, not an interview.</li><li>Conners scales are rating scales mainly for ADHD.</li><li>Vineland measures adaptive behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The K-SADS-PL (Present and Lifetime) version is widely used.</li><li>The SCID is the main adult semi-structured interview.</li><li>Child assessment always uses several informants.</li><li>The first department of psychology in India was started in 1916 at the University of:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 29, "question": "The first department of psychology in India was started in 1916 at the University of:", "options": ["Calcutta", "Mysore", "Bombay", "Madras"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The University of Calcutta started the first department of experimental psychology in 1916, with N.N. Sengupta, influenced by Sir Asutosh Mookerjee.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Mysore started its department in 1924.</li><li>Bombay and Madras came later.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RINPAS 2019 asked in which year the first psychology department in India was established.</li><li>The Indian Psychological Association was formed in 1925.</li><li>Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytic Society in 1922 (JEMAS 2024).</li><li>Arjun was diagnosed with Asperger’s disorder in 2010 under DSM-IV-TR. If he were assessed today using DSM-5-TR, he would most likely be diagnosed with:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — RINPAS 2019. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 30, "question": "Arjun was diagnosed with Asperger’s disorder in 2010 under DSM-IV-TR. If he were assessed today using DSM-5-TR, he would most likely be diagnosed with:", "options": ["Intellectual developmental disorder", "Autism spectrum disorder", "Pervasive developmental disorder", "Social (pragmatic) communication disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 merged the separate pervasive developmental disorders into one diagnosis, autism spectrum disorder, with two symptom domains: social communication and interaction, and restricted, repetitive behaviours.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Pervasive developmental disorder’ was the old DSM-IV umbrella term.</li><li>Social (pragmatic) communication disorder is a new, separate diagnosis without restricted, repetitive behaviours.</li><li>Intellectual developmental disorder is a different diagnosis.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RINPAS 2024 asked which behaviours are NOT included in DSM-5 ASD.</li><li>Rett syndrome was removed from the ASD category because it has a known genetic cause.</li><li>DSM-5 added sensory over- or under-reactivity as a symptom.</li><li>A patient says her mood is ‘very happy’, but she is tearful and looks sad throughout the interview. This is described as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 31, "question": "A patient says her mood is ‘very happy’, but she is tearful and looks sad throughout the interview. This is described as:", "options": ["Euthymic mood", "Labile affect", "Affect incongruent with stated mood", "Blunted affect"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Mood is what the patient reports (subjective); affect is what the examiner observes. When they do not match, the affect is described as incongruent.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Blunted affect is reduced intensity of expression.</li><li>Labile affect changes rapidly and without control.</li><li>Euthymic means normal mood.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Inappropriate affect: emotion does not fit the content (e.g. laughing about a death).</li><li>Range, intensity, stability and appropriateness of affect are noted in the MSE.</li><li>Flat affect is the extreme form of blunting.</li><li>The National Mental Health Survey of India (2015–16) found a current prevalence of mental morbidity of about 10.6% in adults. In a town of 50,000 adults, roughly how many would be expected to have a current mental disorder?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 32, "question": "The National Mental Health Survey of India (2015–16) found a current prevalence of mental morbidity of about 10.6% in adults. In a town of 50,000 adults, roughly how many would be expected to have a current mental disorder?", "options": ["About 10,600", "About 530", "About 53,000", "About 5,300"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> 10.6% of 50,000 = 0.106 × 50,000 = 5,300.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>530 = 1.06% (decimal error).</li><li>10,600 = 21.2% — double the rate.</li><li>53,000 is more than the whole population.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>NMHS 2015–16 was conducted by NIMHANS; lifetime prevalence was about 13.7%.</li><li>It found a large treatment gap (70–92% across disorders).</li><li>Common mental disorders (depression, anxiety) and substance use were the largest groups.</li><li>A: The International Classification of Diseases (ICD) is published by the American Psychiatric Association.  R: The DSM is published by the American Psychiatric Association.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 33, "question": "<p class=\"stem-lead\">Read the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) and choose the correct option:</p><div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">The International Classification of Diseases (ICD) is published by the American Psychiatric Association.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">The DSM is published by the American Psychiatric Association.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A is false: the ICD is published by the World Health Organization. R is true: the American Psychiatric Association publishes the DSM.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A and B are wrong because A is false.</li><li>C is wrong because A is false and R is true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-10 was endorsed in 1990 and came into use from 1994.</li><li>ICD-11 was adopted by the World Health Assembly in 2019.</li><li>MHCA 2017 Section 3 refers to the latest WHO ICD.</li><li>An illiterate 35-year-old farmer needs an intelligence assessment in a rural clinic. Which Indian test is most suitable?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, JEMAS 2024, NFSU 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 34, "question": "An illiterate 35-year-old farmer needs an intelligence assessment in a rural clinic. Which Indian test is most suitable?", "options": ["WAIS-IV verbal subtests", "Bhatia’s Battery of Performance Tests", "Stanford-Binet verbal scale", "MMPI-2"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> C.M. Bhatia’s Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence (1955) is a non-verbal, performance battery (e.g. Koh’s block design, pass-along, pattern drawing) usable with illiterate adults.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Verbal subtests depend on literacy and schooling.</li><li>MMPI-2 is a personality inventory needing reading ability.</li><li>Verbal scales are unsuitable for an illiterate person.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Bhatia’s battery has five subtests including Koh’s Block Design and Alexander’s Pass-Along.</li><li>Raven’s Coloured Progressive Matrices are another non-verbal option.</li><li>Culture-fair testing reduces language bias.</li><li>Match the disorder (List I) with its ICD-11 grouping (List II):</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 35, "question": "Match the disorder (List I) with its ICD-11 grouping (List II): Codes:<div class=\"mt-wrap\"><table class=\"match-table\"><thead><tr><th>List I</th><th>List II</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Avoidant-restrictive food intake disorder</td><td>a. Impulse control disorders</td></tr><tr><td>2. Body dysmorphic disorder</td><td>b. Feeding or eating disorders</td></tr><tr><td>3. Adjustment disorder</td><td>c. Disorders specifically associated with stress</td></tr><tr><td>4. Pyromania</td><td>d. Obsessive-compulsive or related disorders</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "options": ["1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a", "1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a", "1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a", "1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ARFID is a feeding or eating disorder; body dysmorphic disorder is obsessive-compulsive related; adjustment disorder is specifically associated with stress; pyromania is an impulse control disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The other codes swap BDD with ARFID, or adjustment disorder with pyromania.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-11 impulse control disorders also include kleptomania, intermittent explosive disorder and compulsive sexual behaviour disorder.</li><li>ICD-11 OC-related disorders include hypochondriasis and olfactory reference disorder.</li><li>RML 2024 asked who gave the term ‘kleptomania’.</li><li>The first Disability Act came into effect in India in the year:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — RML 2024. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 36, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · JEMAS 2022</span>The first Disability Act came into effect in India in the year:", "options": ["1968", "1990", "1995", "2016"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Mental health laws & RCI", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act, 1995 was India’s first comprehensive disability law (passed in 1995, in force from February 1996). It covered 7 disabilities.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>No disability Act was passed in 1968.</li><li>1990 is not linked to a disability Act in India.</li><li>2016 is the RPwD Act, which replaced the 1995 Act.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>PwD Act 1995 recognised 7 disabilities; RPwD Act 2016 recognises 21.</li><li>The RCI Act, 1992 dealt with professionals, not the rights of persons with disabilities.</li><li>JEMAS 2022 also asked about the aim of the RCI Act.</li><li>🏛 Which exams ask this: Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</li><li>DSM-5-TR introduced expanded guidance on cultural formulation. Which tool is emphasised for systematically assessing cultural context in clinical evaluation?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 37, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IMHANS 2025</span>DSM-5-TR introduced expanded guidance on cultural formulation. Which tool is emphasised for systematically assessing cultural context in clinical evaluation?", "options": ["Cultural Formulation Interview (CFI)", "Cross-Cultural Checklist (CCC)", "Multicultural Assessment Battery (MAB)", "Global Functioning Scale (GFS)"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Cultural Formulation Interview is a 16-question semi-structured interview in DSM-5 Section III that explores how the person and family understand the problem, cultural factors in stress and support, and expectations of help.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The ‘Cross-Cultural Checklist’ is not a DSM tool.</li><li>There is no DSM ‘Multicultural Assessment Battery’.</li><li>The Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF) was dropped in DSM-5.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The CFI has supplementary modules (e.g. for older adults, immigrants, caregivers).</li><li>DSM-5 uses ‘cultural concepts of distress’ instead of ‘culture-bound syndromes’.</li><li>DSM-5-TR added more text on racism and discrimination.</li><li>The Wechsler Adult Performance Intelligence Scale (WAPIS), an Indian adaptation of the WAIS performance tests, was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 38, "question": "The Wechsler Adult Performance Intelligence Scale (WAPIS), an Indian adaptation of the WAIS performance tests, was developed by:", "options": ["V.V. Kamat", "P. Ramalingaswamy", "A.J. Malin", "C.M. Bhatia"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> P. Ramalingaswamy adapted the WAIS performance subtests for Indian adults as the WAPIS, often used where verbal tests are unsuitable.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Malin – MISIC (Indian WISC) and the Indian VSMS.</li><li>Kamat – Binet-Kamat Test.</li><li>Bhatia – Battery of Performance Tests.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Indian adaptations reduce cultural and language bias.</li><li>NFSU 2023 asked about the history of the Wechsler scales.</li><li>Norms for Indian tests often need updating.</li><li>Raju has had hallucinations and delusions for 3 months and is still ill. The clinician diagnoses schizophreniform disorder but cannot yet know if he will recover within 6 months. Which word should be added?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 39, "question": "Raju has had hallucinations and delusions for 3 months and is still ill. The clinician diagnoses schizophreniform disorder but cannot yet know if he will recover within 6 months. Which word should be added?", "options": ["Unspecified", "Other specified", "In partial remission", "Provisional"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ‘Provisional’ is used when the clinician expects full criteria will be met but cannot yet confirm it — for example, schizophreniform disorder before recovery is known.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Unspecified’ is used when the clinician does not give a reason criteria are not met.</li><li>‘Other specified’ records the specific reason criteria are not met.</li><li>He is still ill, so remission does not apply.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Schizophreniform disorder is recorded as ‘provisional’ while the person is still ill.</li><li>‘Other specified’ = the clinician records why criteria are not met.</li><li>Clinical judgement is central in using these labels.</li><li>A: Thomas Szasz argued that mental illness is a ‘myth’.  R: Szasz believed that mental disorders are caused mainly by brain lesions.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 40, "question": "<p class=\"stem-lead\">Read the Assertion (A) and Reason (R) and choose the correct option:</p><div class=\"ar-block\"><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Assertion (A)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Thomas Szasz argued that mental illness is a ‘myth’.</span></div><div class=\"ar-row\"><span class=\"ar-label\">Reason (R)</span><span class=\"ar-text\">Szasz believed that mental disorders are caused mainly by brain lesions.</span></div></div>", "options": ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A", "Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A", "A is true, but R is false", "A is false, but R is true"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> A is true: in The Myth of Mental Illness (1961), Szasz said mental illness is a metaphor for ‘problems in living’. R is false: he argued that only conditions with brain lesions are true diseases, and that most psychiatric conditions are NOT of this kind.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A and B are wrong because R is false.</li><li>D is wrong because A is true.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Szasz was a leading figure of the ‘anti-psychiatry’ movement, with R.D. Laing.</li><li>He opposed involuntary hospitalisation.</li><li>Labelling theory (Scheff) made a related social argument.</li><li>A 7-year-old Marathi-speaking boy is referred to rule out intellectual disability. The most suitable Indian individual intelligence test is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — RINPAS 2019, JANAS. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 41, "question": "A 7-year-old Marathi-speaking boy is referred to rule out intellectual disability. The most suitable Indian individual intelligence test is:", "options": ["Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale", "Binet-Kamat Test", "Raven’s Advanced Progressive Matrices", "Bayley Scales of Infant Development"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Binet-Kamat Test (V.V. Kamat, 1934) is an Indian adaptation of the Stanford-Binet for children, available in Marathi, Kannada and Hindi, and widely used for IQ and ID assessment.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>WAIS is for adults (16+).</li><li>Raven’s Advanced Matrices are for above-average adolescents and adults.</li><li>Bayley scales are for infants and toddlers (up to 42 months).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IMHANS 2021 asked who made the first attempt to standardise mental tests in India.</li><li>MISIC is the Indian adaptation of the WISC (Malin).</li><li>Social maturity is checked alongside IQ with the VSMS.</li><li>In ICD-10, the block F40–F48 is titled:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — IMHANS 2021. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 42, "question": "In ICD-10, the block F40–F48 is titled:", "options": ["Behavioural syndromes associated with physiological disturbances", "Mood (affective) disorders", "Neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders", "Organic, including symptomatic, mental disorders"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> F40–F48 includes phobic anxiety disorders (F40), other anxiety disorders (F41), OCD (F42), reaction to severe stress and adjustment disorders (F43), dissociative (conversion) disorders (F44), somatoform disorders (F45) and neurasthenia (F48.0).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F50–F59 – behavioural syndromes with physiological disturbances (eating, sleep, sexual dysfunction).</li><li>F30–F39 – mood disorders.</li><li>F00–F09 – organic disorders.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F43.1 = PTSD; F43.2 = adjustment disorders.</li><li>F41.0 panic disorder; F41.1 generalised anxiety disorder.</li><li>ICD-11 drops the term ‘neurotic’.</li><li>In an MSE, a patient is asked, ‘What would you do if you found a stamped, addressed envelope on the road?’ This question tests:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 43, "question": "In an MSE, a patient is asked, ‘What would you do if you found a stamped, addressed envelope on the road?’ This question tests:", "options": ["Judgement", "Abstraction", "Memory", "Orientation"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Test judgement is assessed by asking how the person would act in a practical situation; a sensible answer is ‘post it’. Social and personal judgement are also judged from history.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Abstraction is tested with proverbs and similarities.</li><li>Orientation is to time, place and person.</li><li>Memory is tested by recall.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Another test question: ‘What would you do if you smelt smoke in a cinema hall?’</li><li>Poor judgement is common in mania and frontal lobe damage.</li><li>Judgement and insight are usually recorded together.</li><li>Suzanne Kobasa’s ‘hardy’ personality, which buffers people against stress, consists of:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 44, "question": "Suzanne Kobasa’s ‘hardy’ personality, which buffers people against stress, consists of:", "options": ["Competence, confidence and caring", "Control, conformity and compliance", "Calmness, curiosity and courage", "Commitment, control and challenge"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Kobasa (1979) found that executives who stayed healthy under stress showed commitment (involvement), control (belief they can influence events) and challenge (seeing change as an opportunity).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>These are not Kobasa’s three Cs.</li><li>These are not Kobasa’s three Cs.</li><li>Conformity and compliance are not hardiness traits.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Resilience research builds on hardiness.</li><li>Optimism (Scheier and Carver) is another buffer.</li><li>Antonovsky’s ‘sense of coherence’ is a related idea.</li><li>George Engel’s biopsychosocial model (1977) proposes that illness results from:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 45, "question": "George Engel’s biopsychosocial model (1977) proposes that illness results from:", "options": ["The interaction of biological, psychological and social factors", "Social labelling alone, as in labelling theory", "Biological causes alone, as in the biomedical model", "Unconscious conflicts alone, as in psychoanalysis"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Engel criticised the purely biomedical model and argued that biological, psychological and social factors interact in both health and illness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Biological causes alone’ is the biomedical model he criticised.</li><li>Unconscious conflict alone is a narrow psychodynamic view.</li><li>Labelling alone is Scheff’s social view.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The model underlies modern clinical assessment and formulation.</li><li>The WHO ICF also uses a biopsychosocial approach.</li><li>Engel’s paper appeared in Science (1977).</li><li>A woman suddenly develops a psychotic state with changing delusions and hallucinations within 2 weeks of onset, and recovers fully within a month. The ICD-11 diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — IHBAS 2024. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 46, "question": "A woman suddenly develops a psychotic state with changing delusions and hallucinations within 2 weeks of onset, and recovers fully within a month. The ICD-11 diagnosis is:", "options": ["Schizoaffective disorder", "Acute and transient psychotic disorder", "Schizophrenia", "Delusional disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 acute and transient psychotic disorder (6A23): sudden onset (within 2 weeks), rapidly changing symptoms, and usually full remission within about 3 months (often within days to a month).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Schizophrenia needs symptoms for at least 1 month in ICD-11.</li><li>Schizoaffective disorder needs a concurrent mood episode.</li><li>Delusional disorder involves stable delusions for at least 3 months.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 equivalent: brief psychotic disorder (1 day to 1 month).</li><li>ICD-10 code: F23 (acute and transient psychotic disorders).</li><li>ATPD is more common in women and in developing countries, including India.</li><li>A screening test for a rare condition (1 in 1,000 people) is 95% accurate. Most people who screen positive will still not have the condition. This problem is due to the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 47, "question": "A screening test for a rare condition (1 in 1,000 people) is 95% accurate. Most people who screen positive will still not have the condition. This problem is due to the:", "options": ["Low internal consistency of the test", "Ceiling effect of the test", "Restriction of range in the sample", "Low base rate of the condition"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> When a condition is rare, even an accurate test produces many more false positives than true positives. Meehl and Rosen (1955) warned clinicians to consider base rates.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Internal consistency is reliability, not the cause here.</li><li>A ceiling effect happens when many people score at the top.</li><li>Restriction of range lowers correlations; it does not explain false positives.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Positive predictive value falls as the base rate falls.</li><li>This is why screening is followed by a confirmatory assessment.</li><li>GMCH 2023 asked how sensitivity is defined.</li><li>In ICD-11, ‘acute stress reaction’ is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 48, "question": "In ICD-11, ‘acute stress reaction’ is:", "options": ["A subtype of post-traumatic stress disorder", "Classified along with the dissociative disorders", "Not a mental disorder; listed under factors influencing health", "Placed in the new chapter on sleep-wake disorders"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 sees acute stress reaction as a normal response to an extreme stressor, so it moved it out of the mental disorders chapter to ‘Factors influencing health status or contact with health services’ (QE84).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>It is not a subtype of PTSD.</li><li>It is not a dissociative disorder.</li><li>Sleep-wake disorders are a separate chapter.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 still has acute stress disorder as a mental disorder (3 days to 1 month).</li><li>ICD-11 ‘disorders specifically associated with stress’: PTSD, complex PTSD, prolonged grief, adjustment disorder, RAD, DSED.</li><li>Burnout is also coded under factors influencing health status (QD85), not as a mental disorder.</li><li>India’s National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) was launched in:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 49, "question": "India’s National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) was launched in:", "options": ["1982", "1987", "1996", "2017"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The NMHP began in 1982 to make basic mental healthcare available and accessible to all, especially the poor, by integrating it with general healthcare.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>1987 – the Mental Health Act was passed.</li><li>1996 – the District Mental Health Programme (DMHP) was started under NMHP.</li><li>2017 – MHCA was passed.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The DMHP grew from the Bellary model (Karnataka, 1985) developed by NIMHANS.</li><li>Tele-MANAS (tele-mental health) was launched in 2022.</li><li>India’s first National Mental Health Policy was released in 2014.</li><li>ICD-11 divides bipolar disorders into:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 50, "question": "ICD-11 divides bipolar disorders into:", "options": ["Manic episode, depressive episode and mixed episode", "Bipolar disorder with and without psychosis only", "Only bipolar affective disorder and cyclothymia", "Bipolar type I disorder, bipolar type II disorder and cyclothymic disorder"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 (6A60 bipolar type I, 6A61 bipolar type II, 6A62 cyclothymic disorder) follows the DSM approach. ICD-10 had only ‘bipolar affective disorder’ (F31), with bipolar II under ‘other bipolar affective disorders’.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>That was closer to ICD-10, which had no separate bipolar II diagnosis.</li><li>Episodes are not the diagnostic categories.</li><li>Psychotic symptoms are recorded as specifiers.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-10 needed at least two episodes for bipolar affective disorder; ICD-11 needs only one manic or mixed episode for type I.</li><li>ICD-11 kept ‘mixed episode’, unlike DSM-5.</li><li>Single and recurrent depressive disorder: 6A70 and 6A71.</li><li>Section 95 of MHCA 2017 prohibits all of the following, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 51, "question": "Section 95 of MHCA 2017 prohibits all of the following, EXCEPT:", "options": ["ECT without muscle relaxants and anaesthesia", "Modified ECT given with informed consent", "Sterilisation as a treatment for mental illness", "Chaining in any manner"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Mental health laws & RCI", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Section 95 bans unmodified ECT, ECT for minors (except with guardian consent and Board permission), sterilisation as a treatment for mental illness, and chaining. Modified ECT (with anaesthesia and muscle relaxants) given with informed consent is allowed.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Unmodified (direct) ECT is banned.</li><li>Sterilisation as treatment is banned.</li><li>Chaining in any form is banned.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Section 96: psychosurgery needs the person’s informed consent and Board approval.</li><li>Section 97: seclusion and solitary confinement are not allowed.</li><li>Restraint must be recorded, reported monthly to the Board, and the nominated representative told within 24 hours.</li><li>🏛 Which exams ask this: Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</li><li>ICD-11 officially came into effect on:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, JEMAS 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 52, "question": "ICD-11 officially came into effect on:", "options": ["1 January 2024", "1 May 2018", "1 January 2022", "1 January 2019"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The World Health Assembly adopted ICD-11 in May 2019, and it came into effect for recording and reporting on 1 January 2022.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>2019 is when it was adopted, not when it took effect.</li><li>2024 is when the CDDR (clinical guidelines) were released.</li><li>May 2018 is not linked to ICD-11 coming into effect.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-11 is fully digital, with an online browser and coding tool.</li><li>Countries move to ICD-11 at their own pace.</li><li>ICD-11 has 26 main chapters plus supplementary sections.</li><li>The House-Tree-Person (HTP) projective drawing test was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, JEMAS 2024, NFSU 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 53, "question": "The House-Tree-Person (HTP) projective drawing test was developed by:", "options": ["Hermann Rorschach", "Henry Murray", "John Buck", "Karen Machover"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> John Buck introduced the House-Tree-Person test in 1948; the house, tree and person are interpreted as reflecting home life, the inner self and relationships.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Machover – Draw-a-Person.</li><li>Murray – Thematic Apperception Test (with Morgan).</li><li>Rorschach – inkblot test.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Drawing tests are useful with children who speak little.</li><li>Scoring is largely qualitative.</li><li>They are best used as a starting point for discussion.</li><li>In ICD-10, the block F30–F39 covers:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 54, "question": "In ICD-10, the block F30–F39 covers:", "options": ["Mood (affective) disorders", "Organic mental disorders", "Disorders of adult personality and behaviour", "Neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> F30–F39 are mood disorders: F30 manic episode, F31 bipolar affective disorder, F32 depressive episode, F33 recurrent depressive disorder, F34 persistent mood disorders (cyclothymia, dysthymia).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F40–F48 – neurotic, stress-related and somatoform.</li><li>F60–F69 – adult personality and behaviour.</li><li>F00–F09 – organic disorders.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F34.0 cyclothymia; F34.1 dysthymia.</li><li>In ICD-11, mood disorders are 6A60–6A8Z.</li><li>F38 other mood disorders; F39 unspecified mood disorder.</li><li>A man with chronic schizophrenia applies for a disability certificate. The medical board will assess his disability using the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 55, "question": "A man with chronic schizophrenia applies for a disability certificate. The medical board will assess his disability using the:", "options": ["Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale", "Indian Scale for Assessment of Autism (ISAA)", "Vineland Social Maturity Scale (VSMS)", "Indian Disability Evaluation and Assessment Scale (IDEAS)"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Mental health laws & RCI", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> IDEAS is the government-notified tool for measuring disability due to mental illness. It rates self-care, interpersonal activities, communication and understanding, and work, and also takes duration of illness into account.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ISAA is for autism spectrum disorder.</li><li>VSMS measures social maturity, used with IQ for intellectual disability.</li><li>An IQ test does not measure disability from mental illness.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>IDEAS was developed by the Indian Psychiatric Society’s Rehabilitation Committee (2002).</li><li>JEMAS 2024 asked the full form of ISAA.</li><li>Certification is done by a medical board that includes a psychiatrist.</li><li>🏛 Which exams ask this: Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</li><li>In ICD-11, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder is placed in:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, IHBAS 2024. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 56, "question": "In ICD-11, attention deficit hyperactivity disorder is placed in:", "options": ["Mood disorders", "Neurodevelopmental disorders", "Impulse control disorders", "Disruptive behaviour or dissocial disorders"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 places ADHD (6A05) with the neurodevelopmental disorders. ICD-10 called it ‘hyperkinetic disorder’ (F90) and placed it with behavioural and emotional disorders of childhood.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Disruptive behaviour or dissocial disorders include oppositional defiant and conduct-dissocial disorder.</li><li>Impulse control disorders include pyromania and kleptomania.</li><li>ADHD is not a mood disorder.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-11 also lets ADHD be diagnosed with autism.</li><li>ICD-11 neurodevelopmental group: intellectual development, speech/language, ASD, learning, motor, ADHD, stereotyped movement.</li><li>DSM-5 also placed ADHD in the neurodevelopmental chapter.</li><li>The aim of the __________ was to regulate training policies and programmes in the field of disability rehabilitation and to standardise training courses for professionals working with people with disabilities.</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 57, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · JEMAS 2022</span>The aim of the __________ was to regulate training policies and programmes in the field of disability rehabilitation and to standardise training courses for professionals working with people with disabilities.", "options": ["RPwD Act 2016", "RCI Act 1992", "PwD Act 1995", "MHCA 2017"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Mental health laws & RCI", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 set up RCI to regulate and monitor training of rehabilitation professionals, standardise their courses, and maintain a register of qualified professionals.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RPwD Act 2016 – rights and entitlements of persons with disabilities.</li><li>PwD Act 1995 – the earlier disability rights law (7 disabilities).</li><li>MHCA 2017 – rights of persons with mental illness and mental healthcare.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RCI regulates clinical psychologists, rehabilitation psychologists, special educators, audiologists and others.</li><li>Practising as a rehabilitation professional without RCI registration is an offence.</li><li>RCI also promotes research in rehabilitation and special education.</li><li>🏛 Which exams ask this: Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</li><li>For 3 weeks, 12-year-old Aman has been irritable and angry most of the day, lost interest in cricket, sleeps poorly and says he is useless. Under DSM-5, can major depressive disorder be considered?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 58, "question": "For 3 weeks, 12-year-old Aman has been irritable and angry most of the day, lost interest in cricket, sleeps poorly and says he is useless. Under DSM-5, can major depressive disorder be considered?", "options": ["No — children must report sadness, not irritability", "Yes — but only if symptoms have lasted 2 years", "No — depression cannot be diagnosed before 18", "Yes — in children, irritable mood can replace depressed mood"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 allows irritable mood instead of depressed mood in children and adolescents. With 2 weeks of symptoms and enough criteria, MDD can be diagnosed.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Irritability is accepted in children.</li><li>Depression can be diagnosed at any age.</li><li>2 years is for persistent depressive disorder in adults (1 year in children).</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Children may show more somatic complaints and irritability.</li><li>Rule out DMDD if outbursts are severe and chronic.</li><li>CBT and interpersonal therapy for adolescents are effective.</li><li>Which version of the DSM is currently in use (as of 2025)?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 59, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IHBAS 2025</span>Which version of the DSM is currently in use (as of 2025)?", "options": ["DSM-IV", "DSM-V", "DSM-III", "DSM-6"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The current edition is DSM-5 (written with an Arabic ‘5’), in its text-revised form DSM-5-TR (2022). ‘DSM-V’ in the options means DSM-5.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-IV (1994) and DSM-IV-TR (2000) have been replaced.</li><li>DSM-III dates from 1980.</li><li>There is no DSM-6 yet.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>NFSU 2023 asked which manual’s text revision currently frames psychopathology criteria.</li><li>J&amp;K 2023 asked who publishes the DSM (American Psychiatric Association).</li><li>DSM-5-TR was published in March 2022.</li><li>Which of the following is a key legal provision under the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 60, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IHBAS 2025</span>Which of the following is a key legal provision under the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017?", "options": ["Suicide attempts are decriminalised, recognising mental illness as a contributing factor", "Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) can be administered without consent in all cases", "Only psychiatrists can provide therapy; psychologists are excluded", "Mental health establishments are exempt from registration if government-run"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Mental health laws & RCI", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Section 115 of MHCA 2017 says that a person who attempts suicide shall be presumed, unless proved otherwise, to have severe stress and shall not be tried or punished. The government must provide care and rehabilitation instead.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ECT needs informed consent; unmodified ECT (without anaesthesia and muscle relaxants) is banned.</li><li>Clinical psychologists are recognised ‘mental health professionals’ under the Act.</li><li>All mental health establishments, including government ones, must be registered.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Section 115 means attempted suicide is treated as a health problem needing care, not as a crime.</li><li>Section 29 asks the government to plan suicide prevention programmes.</li><li>MHCA 2017 is rights-based and follows the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (UNCRPD).</li><li>🏛 Which exams ask this: Exams like IHBAS, JEMAS and RINPAS have asked about mental health laws, RCI and disability Acts — usually one simple question per paper — while NIMHANS and CIP rarely ask them. Keep the basics of MHCA 2017, the RCI Act and the RPwD Act ready.</li><li>For 6 weeks, 8-year-old Riya has refused to go to school, cries when her mother leaves, and worries that her mother will have an accident. She has stomach aches on school mornings. The most likely diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 61, "question": "For 6 weeks, 8-year-old Riya has refused to go to school, cries when her mother leaves, and worries that her mother will have an accident. She has stomach aches on school mornings. The most likely diagnosis is:", "options": ["Generalised anxiety disorder", "Specific phobia (school)", "Separation anxiety disorder", "Social anxiety disorder"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Separation anxiety disorder: excessive fear about separation from attachment figures, worry about harm to them, refusal to go out or to school, and physical complaints — lasting at least 4 weeks in children.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The fear is about separation from the mother, not school itself.</li><li>GAD involves worry about many areas.</li><li>Social anxiety is fear of being judged by others.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RINPAS 2024 asked what separation anxiety is part of.</li><li>In DSM-5, separation anxiety disorder is in the anxiety disorders chapter and can be diagnosed in adults (6 months).</li><li>Treatment: CBT with gradual return to school and parent involvement.</li><li>Arrange the stages of Hans Selye’s General Adaptation Syndrome in order:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — RINPAS 2024, M.Phil Entrance 2021, JEPAS 2020. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 62, "question": "Arrange the stages of Hans Selye’s General Adaptation Syndrome in order: I. Resistance II. Alarm reaction III. Exhaustion", "options": ["I, II, III", "II, III, I", "III, II, I", "II, I, III"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Alarm reaction (fight-or-flight) → Resistance (the body adapts and copes) → Exhaustion (resources run out and illness becomes more likely).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Alarm comes first and exhaustion last.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RML 2024 asked for the correct order of these stages.</li><li>Selye also coined ‘eustress’ and ‘distress’.</li><li>The HPA axis drives the resistance stage.</li><li>The WHO’s mental health Gap Action Programme (mhGAP), launched in 2008, mainly aims to:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 63, "question": "The WHO’s mental health Gap Action Programme (mhGAP), launched in 2008, mainly aims to:", "options": ["Replace the ICD with a new mental health classification", "Close all psychiatric hospitals in member states within five years", "Scale up care for priority conditions through non-specialist workers", "Train only psychiatrists working in high-income countries"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> mhGAP aims to reduce the treatment gap in low- and middle-income countries, using an Intervention Guide that helps non-specialist health workers detect and manage priority conditions such as depression, psychosis, epilepsy and substance use.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>It focuses on non-specialists in low- and middle-income countries.</li><li>It is not a classification system.</li><li>It does not set deadlines to close hospitals.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The mhGAP Intervention Guide (mhGAP-IG) was released in 2010 (version 2.0 in 2016).</li><li>India’s NMHS found a treatment gap of 70–92%.</li><li>Task-shifting to lay health workers is a key strategy.</li><li>For two years Sunil has had stomach pain, tiredness and back pain. He is preoccupied with these symptoms and visits many doctors, although all tests are normal. The ICD-11 diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 64, "question": "For two years Sunil has had stomach pain, tiredness and back pain. He is preoccupied with these symptoms and visits many doctors, although all tests are normal. The ICD-11 diagnosis is:", "options": ["Bodily distress disorder", "Hypochondriasis", "Somatisation disorder", "Dissociative neurological symptom disorder"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-11 bodily distress disorder (6C20): distressing bodily symptoms with excessive attention to them, such as repeated medical visits. It replaced most ICD-10 somatoform disorders and is similar to DSM-5’s somatic symptom disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Somatisation disorder’ is the ICD-10 term (F45.0), not used in ICD-11.</li><li>Hypochondriasis is preoccupation with having a serious disease, often with few symptoms; ICD-11 places it with the OC-related disorders.</li><li>Dissociative neurological symptom disorder involves neurological-type symptoms such as paralysis or seizures.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Severity: mild, moderate, severe.</li><li>ICD-11 also introduced ‘body integrity dysphoria’.</li><li>Somatoform disorders in ICD-10 were coded F45.</li><li>Thomas Scheff’s labelling theory of mental illness proposes that:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — NFSU 2023, RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 65, "question": "Thomas Scheff’s labelling theory of mental illness proposes that:", "options": ["Mental illness is caused mainly by faulty genes", "Mental illness results from irrational beliefs", "Labels always improve treatment", "Being labelled mentally ill pushes a person into the role of a patient"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Scheff (1966) argued that once a person is labelled ‘mentally ill’, others respond to the label and the person may come to act in line with it, so the label helps keep the behaviour going.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Genetic causes are a biological view.</li><li>Labelling theory warns that labels can harm.</li><li>Irrational beliefs are Ellis’s cognitive view.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Rosenhan’s 1973 study is often cited in support of labelling effects.</li><li>Stigma research builds on labelling theory.</li><li>Szasz argued mental illness is a ‘myth’.</li><li>In DSM-5, ‘dementia’ was largely replaced by the term:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 66, "question": "In DSM-5, ‘dementia’ was largely replaced by the term:", "options": ["Major neurocognitive disorder", "Amnestic disorder", "Delirium", "Organic brain syndrome"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 introduced ‘major neurocognitive disorder’ (in place of dementia) and a new ‘mild neurocognitive disorder’ for smaller declines. The term ‘dementia’ can still be used within the subtypes.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Delirium remains a separate neurocognitive disorder.</li><li>Amnestic disorder was folded into major or mild NCD.</li><li>‘Organic brain syndrome’ is an old term no longer used.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Major vs mild NCD depends on whether the decline interferes with independence in everyday activities.</li><li>Subtypes are listed by cause, e.g. due to Alzheimer’s disease, vascular, Lewy body.</li><li>ICD-11 codes dementia due to Alzheimer disease as 6D80.</li><li>‘Type D’ personality, linked with poor outcomes in heart disease, combines:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 67, "question": "‘Type D’ personality, linked with poor outcomes in heart disease, combines:", "options": ["Optimism and extraversion", "Commitment, control and challenge", "Negative affectivity and social inhibition", "Hostility and time urgency"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Johan Denollet described Type D (‘distressed’) personality: a tendency to feel negative emotions plus inhibition in expressing them socially.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Hostility and time urgency describe Type A.</li><li>Commitment, control and challenge are Kobasa’s hardiness.</li><li>Optimism and extraversion are protective traits.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Hostility is the toxic part of Type A for heart disease.</li><li>Type C has been linked to emotional suppression and cancer, with weak evidence.</li><li>Type B is relaxed and easy-going.</li><li>Match the pioneer (List I) with the contribution (List II):</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 68, "question": "Match the pioneer (List I) with the contribution (List II): Codes:<div class=\"mt-wrap\"><table class=\"match-table\"><thead><tr><th>List I</th><th>List II</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Lightner Witmer</td><td>a. Mental hygiene movement (‘A Mind That Found Itself’)</td></tr><tr><td>2. Philippe Pinel</td><td>b. Campaign for state mental hospitals in the USA</td></tr><tr><td>3. Clifford Beers</td><td>c. First psychological clinic (1896)</td></tr><tr><td>4. Dorothea Dix</td><td>d. Unchaining patients and moral treatment in Paris</td></tr></tbody></table></div>", "options": ["1-d, 2-c, 3-a, 4-b", "1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b", "1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a", "1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Witmer – first psychological clinic (University of Pennsylvania, 1896); Pinel – removed chains and introduced moral treatment (Bicêtre and Salpêtrière, 1790s); Beers – started the mental hygiene movement after his book (1908); Dix – campaigned for state mental hospitals in the USA.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The other codes swap Witmer with Pinel, or Beers with Dix.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>William Tuke opened the York Retreat in England (1796), also based on moral treatment.</li><li>Witmer also coined the term ‘clinical psychology’ (1907).</li><li>Benjamin Rush is called the father of American psychiatry.</li><li>DSM-5 replaced the ‘mixed episode’ of bipolar disorder with:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — GMCH 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 69, "question": "DSM-5 replaced the ‘mixed episode’ of bipolar disorder with:", "options": ["The ‘with mixed features’ specifier", "A new diagnosis called mixed bipolar disorder", "The ‘rapid cycling’ specifier", "Cyclothymic disorder"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-IV required full criteria for both mania and depression at once (a ‘mixed episode’). DSM-5 replaced this with a ‘with mixed features’ specifier that can be added to manic, hypomanic or depressive episodes.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>No separate ‘mixed bipolar disorder’ was created.</li><li>Rapid cycling is about the number of episodes per year.</li><li>Cyclothymia is a separate long-term condition.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 also added the ‘with anxious distress’ specifier.</li><li>Bipolar disorders got their own chapter in DSM-5.</li><li>Increased energy or activity became a core criterion for mania in DSM-5.</li><li>Before her exams, Priya makes a study timetable and asks her teacher to explain difficult topics. She is using:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 70, "question": "Before her exams, Priya makes a study timetable and asks her teacher to explain difficult topics. She is using:", "options": ["Defensive denial", "Emotion-focused coping", "Avoidance coping", "Problem-focused coping"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Problem-focused coping acts directly on the stressor — planning, seeking information or help. Emotion-focused coping manages the feelings (e.g. relaxation, venting).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Emotion-focused coping deals with feelings rather than the problem.</li><li>Avoidance means staying away from the problem.</li><li>Denial refuses to accept the problem.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Problem-focused coping suits controllable stressors; emotion-focused suits uncontrollable ones.</li><li>Seeking social support can serve both functions.</li><li>Lazarus and Folkman described these two main types.</li><li>DSM-5 was published by the American Psychiatric Association in:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, IHBAS 2024, NFSU 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 71, "question": "DSM-5 was published by the American Psychiatric Association in:", "options": ["2000", "2010", "2013", "2022"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 was released in May 2013. Its text revision, DSM-5-TR, followed in March 2022.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>2000 – DSM-IV-TR.</li><li>2010 – drafts were released for comment, but no edition was published.</li><li>2022 – DSM-5-TR.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>J&amp;K 2023 asked who publishes the DSM (the American Psychiatric Association).</li><li>NFSU 2023 asked which manual’s text revision currently frames psychopathology criteria (DSM-5).</li><li>ICD is published by the WHO.</li><li>In a mental status examination, the ‘serial 7s’ task (subtracting 7 from 100 repeatedly) mainly assesses:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 72, "question": "In a mental status examination, the ‘serial 7s’ task (subtracting 7 from 100 repeatedly) mainly assesses:", "options": ["Judgement", "Attention and concentration", "Abstract thinking", "Long-term memory"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Serial 7s (or serial 3s, or spelling ‘WORLD’ backwards) tests the ability to sustain attention and concentrate.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Long-term memory is tested with past personal or general events.</li><li>Abstraction is tested with proverbs and similarities.</li><li>Judgement is tested with practical situations.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Digit span forward is another attention test.</li><li>Low education can lower serial-7 scores, so serial 3s may be used.</li><li>Attention problems are prominent in delirium.</li><li>Arrange these editions of the DSM in the order in which they were published:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 73, "question": "Arrange these editions of the DSM in the order in which they were published: I. DSM-III II. DSM-I III. DSM-5 IV. DSM-IV", "options": ["I, II, IV, III", "II, IV, I, III", "II, I, IV, III", "II, I, III, IV"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-I (1952) → DSM-III (1980) → DSM-IV (1994) → DSM-5 (2013). (DSM-II came in 1968, DSM-III-R in 1987, DSM-IV-TR in 2000, DSM-5-TR in 2022.)</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-I is the oldest, and DSM-5 is the newest of these four.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-III (1980) introduced explicit diagnostic criteria and the multiaxial system.</li><li>DSM-5 switched from Roman to Arabic numerals.</li><li>IHBAS 2025 asked which version of DSM is currently in use.</li><li>To assess the intellectual functioning of a 70-year-old English-speaking retired teacher, the most appropriate test is the:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 74, "question": "To assess the intellectual functioning of a 70-year-old English-speaking retired teacher, the most appropriate test is the:", "options": ["Binet-Kamat Test", "Bayley Scales", "Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC)", "Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The WAIS covers adults from 16 to 90 years and is the standard individual adult intelligence test.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>WISC is for children aged 6–16.</li><li>Binet-Kamat is mainly for children.</li><li>Bayley is for infants and toddlers.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>WAIS-IV gives four indexes: verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning, working memory and processing speed.</li><li>JEMAS 2024 asked about WAIS-IV index scales.</li><li>WPPSI is for preschool children.</li><li>In ICD-10, the block F70–F79 covers:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 75, "question": "In ICD-10, the block F70–F79 covers:", "options": ["Adult personality disorders", "Mental retardation", "Disorders of psychological development", "Schizophrenia and related disorders"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> F70 mild, F71 moderate, F72 severe, F73 profound mental retardation (F78 other, F79 unspecified).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F80–F89 – disorders of psychological development.</li><li>F20–F29 – schizophrenia and related disorders.</li><li>F60–F69 – adult personality and behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ICD-10 IQ ranges: mild 50–69, moderate 35–49, severe 20–34, profound below 20.</li><li>ICD-11 uses ‘disorders of intellectual development’ (6A00).</li><li>IMHANS 2021 asked the DSM-IV-TR IQ criterion for mental retardation (about 2 SD below the mean).</li><li>Under DSM-5, enuresis is diagnosed only if the child’s chronological or developmental age is at least:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 76, "question": "Under DSM-5, enuresis is diagnosed only if the child’s chronological or developmental age is at least:", "options": ["5 years", "3 years", "4 years", "7 years"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Enuresis needs repeated voiding of urine into bed or clothes at least twice a week for 3 months, in a child at least 5 years old (or equivalent developmental level).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>3 years is too young; bladder control is still developing.</li><li>4 years is the cut-off for encopresis.</li><li>7 years is not the DSM-5 rule.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>M.Phil Entrance 2021 had enuresis as an answer option.</li><li>Types: nocturnal only, diurnal only, both.</li><li>Primary enuresis = never dry; secondary = after a dry period.</li><li>A man counts his steps while walking, but this causes him no distress and does not affect his work, family or social life. Under DSM-5:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — RML 2024, M.Phil Entrance 2021. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 77, "question": "A man counts his steps while walking, but this causes him no distress and does not affect his work, family or social life. Under DSM-5:", "options": ["No disorder is diagnosed, as the clinical significance criterion is not met", "Other specified obsessive-compulsive disorder is diagnosed", "Mild obsessive-compulsive disorder is diagnosed", "Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is diagnosed"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Most DSM-5 diagnoses need the symptoms to cause clinically significant distress or impairment in social, occupational or other important areas. Without this, the behaviour is a harmless habit, not a disorder.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>OCD needs time-consuming or distressing obsessions or compulsions.</li><li>OCPD is a pervasive pattern of perfectionism and control, causing problems.</li><li>‘Other specified’ still needs clinically significant distress or impairment.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>This criterion was added widely in DSM-IV (1994).</li><li>The DSM-5 definition of mental disorder also excludes expected responses to common stressors such as loss.</li><li>Socially deviant behaviour alone is not a mental disorder.</li><li>An observer divides a 30-minute classroom period into 10-second blocks and records only whether a child’s hand-flapping occurs in each block. This method is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 78, "question": "An observer divides a 30-minute classroom period into 10-second blocks and records only whether a child’s hand-flapping occurs in each block. This method is:", "options": ["Event recording (frequency count)", "Duration recording", "Interval (time) sampling", "Anecdotal recording"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Interval sampling records whether a behaviour occurred during each short interval; the result is the percentage of intervals with the behaviour.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Event recording counts every instance of the behaviour.</li><li>Duration recording measures how long the behaviour lasts.</li><li>Anecdotal records are narrative notes.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Momentary time sampling notes behaviour only at the end of each interval.</li><li>Inter-observer agreement checks the reliability of observation.</li><li>Behavioural observation is a core part of child assessment.</li><li>A 7-year-old has trouble using language socially — he does not greet people, interrupts, and cannot adjust his speech to the listener — but has no restricted or repetitive behaviours. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 79, "question": "A 7-year-old has trouble using language socially — he does not greet people, interrupts, and cannot adjust his speech to the listener — but has no restricted or repetitive behaviours. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:", "options": ["Language disorder", "Selective mutism", "Autism spectrum disorder, level 1", "Social (pragmatic) communication disorder"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Social (pragmatic) communication disorder, new in DSM-5, covers persistent difficulty in the social use of verbal and non-verbal communication without the restricted, repetitive behaviours needed for autism.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ASD also requires restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour.</li><li>Language disorder is about vocabulary and grammar, not social use.</li><li>Selective mutism is failure to speak in specific situations, though speech is normal elsewhere.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>It is placed with the communication disorders in the neurodevelopmental chapter.</li><li>Many children earlier labelled PDD-NOS may now fit this diagnosis.</li><li>Onset is in the early developmental period.</li><li>A patient with schizophrenia accepts that he is ill but insists the illness is caused by black magic done by his neighbours. His level of insight is best graded as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 80, "question": "A patient with schizophrenia accepts that he is ill but insists the illness is caused by black magic done by his neighbours. His level of insight is best graded as:", "options": ["Grade 6 — true emotional insight", "Grade 3 — aware of illness but blames external factors", "Grade 1 — complete denial of illness", "Grade 5 — intellectual insight"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> In the six-point insight scale: 1 complete denial; 2 slight awareness while still denying; 3 awareness but blaming external factors; 4 awareness that the cause is something unknown in oneself; 5 intellectual insight; 6 true emotional insight.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>He does accept he is ill, so it is not complete denial.</li><li>Intellectual insight means accepting the illness and its internal cause without changing behaviour.</li><li>True emotional insight leads to real change in attitudes and behaviour.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RML 2022 asked about the grades of insight.</li><li>Insight is assessed in every MSE.</li><li>Poor insight predicts poor treatment adherence.</li><li>In DSM-5, the three-level insight specifier (good or fair / poor / absent insight-delusional beliefs) is given to OCD and also to:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 81, "question": "In DSM-5, the three-level insight specifier (good or fair / poor / absent insight-delusional beliefs) is given to OCD and also to:", "options": ["Delusional disorder and schizophrenia", "Body dysmorphic disorder and hoarding disorder", "Trichotillomania and excoriation disorder", "Illness anxiety disorder and somatic symptom disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 gives the same insight specifier to OCD, body dysmorphic disorder and hoarding disorder. Full conviction is recorded as ‘absent insight/delusional beliefs’ instead of adding a psychotic diagnosis.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Trichotillomania and excoriation disorder do not have this specifier.</li><li>Illness anxiety and somatic symptom disorders are in a different chapter without it.</li><li>Psychotic disorders do not use an insight specifier.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>BDD also has a ‘with muscle dysmorphia’ specifier.</li><li>Hoarding has a ‘with excessive acquisition’ specifier.</li><li>OCD also has a ‘tic-related’ specifier.</li><li>Parent management training, which teaches parents to reward good behaviour and use consistent consequences, is a first-line treatment for:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 82, "question": "Parent management training, which teaches parents to reward good behaviour and use consistent consequences, is a first-line treatment for:", "options": ["Autism spectrum disorder", "Childhood-onset schizophrenia", "Selective mutism", "Oppositional and conduct problems"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Child & adolescent clinical psychology", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Parent management training (developed from Gerald Patterson’s work) changes the coercive parent–child cycles that maintain defiant and aggressive behaviour.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>ASD needs specialised behavioural and communication interventions.</li><li>Childhood-onset schizophrenia needs medication and psychosocial care.</li><li>Selective mutism is treated with graded exposure and CBT.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Patterson’s ‘coercion theory’ explains how arguments escalate.</li><li>Programmes include Incredible Years and PCIT (parent–child interaction therapy).</li><li>JEMAS 2022 asked what is associated with conduct disorder in adolescence.</li><li>Catatonia is classified in DSM-5 as:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, RML 2022, JEMAS 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 83, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IHBAS 2025</span>Catatonia is classified in DSM-5 as:", "options": ["An independent disorder", "A specifier across several disorders", "A personality disorder", "A subtype of bipolar disorder"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 treats catatonia as a specifier that can be added to psychotic, bipolar, depressive and other disorders (and as ‘catatonic disorder due to another medical condition’ or ‘unspecified catatonia’). At least 3 of 12 signs are needed.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>It is not one fixed stand-alone disorder; it is used across diagnoses.</li><li>It is not a personality disorder.</li><li>It is not limited to bipolar disorder.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 dropped ‘catatonic schizophrenia’ along with the other subtypes.</li><li>ICD-11 has a separate catatonia grouping (6A40–6A41).</li><li>Catatonia responds well to lorazepam and ECT.</li><li>Rosenhan’s study ‘On Being Sane in Insane Places’ (1973) showed that:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 84, "question": "Rosenhan’s study ‘On Being Sane in Insane Places’ (1973) showed that:", "options": ["Medication worked better than psychotherapy for psychosis", "Hospital staff quickly identified the fake patients as healthy", "Healthy pseudo-patients were admitted and kept their labels despite normal behaviour", "Psychiatric diagnosis was found to be almost perfectly reliable across hospitals"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Eight healthy people reported hearing voices once, were admitted (mostly diagnosed with schizophrenia), behaved normally afterwards, and were discharged as ‘schizophrenia in remission’. Staff did not detect them, though some patients did.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The study questioned the reliability of diagnosis.</li><li>Staff failed to detect the pseudo-patients.</li><li>It did not compare medication and therapy.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The study fuelled reforms that led to explicit criteria in DSM-III.</li><li>Its methods and honesty have since been questioned (Cahalan, 2019).</li><li>It is often linked with labelling theory.</li><li>As per the ICD-10 classification of mental disorders, the diagnostic code for schizophrenia is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 85, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · JEMAS 2024</span>As per the ICD-10 classification of mental disorders, the diagnostic code for schizophrenia is:", "options": ["F20", "F90", "F00", "F22"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> F20 is schizophrenia in ICD-10 (F20.0 paranoid, F20.1 hebephrenic, F20.2 catatonic, and so on).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F90 – hyperkinetic disorders (ADHD).</li><li>F00 – dementia in Alzheimer’s disease.</li><li>F22 – persistent delusional disorders.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F20–F29: schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders.</li><li>F21 schizotypal disorder; F23 acute and transient psychotic disorders; F25 schizoaffective disorders; F29 unspecified nonorganic psychosis.</li><li>ICD-11 code for schizophrenia: 6A20.</li><li>Arrange these milestones in clinical psychology in chronological order:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 86, "question": "Arrange these milestones in clinical psychology in chronological order: I. Boulder Conference (scientist-practitioner model) II. Lightner Witmer’s first psychological clinic III. Vail Conference (practitioner-scholar model) IV. Engel’s biopsychosocial model", "options": ["I, II, III, IV", "II, III, I, IV", "II, I, III, IV", "II, I, IV, III"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Witmer’s clinic at the University of Pennsylvania (1896) → Boulder Conference (1949) → Vail Conference (1973) → Engel’s biopsychosocial model (1977).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Witmer (1896) came first; Boulder (1949) before Vail (1973); Engel (1977) is the latest.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The Boulder model trains clinicians as both scientists and practitioners (PhD).</li><li>The Vail model led to the PsyD degree.</li><li>Witmer is often called the ‘father of clinical psychology’.</li><li>In ICD-11, the term ‘disorders of intellectual development’ replaced the ICD-10 term:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — GMCH 2023, RML 2022, M.Phil Entrance 2021. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 87, "question": "In ICD-11, the term ‘disorders of intellectual development’ replaced the ICD-10 term:", "options": ["Mental retardation", "Organic mental disorders", "Specific developmental disorders", "Pervasive developmental disorders"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> ICD-10 used ‘mental retardation’ (F70–F79). ICD-11 uses ‘disorders of intellectual development’ (6A00), with mild, moderate, severe and profound levels.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Specific developmental disorders were learning and speech disorders (F80–F83).</li><li>Pervasive developmental disorders (F84) became autism spectrum disorder in ICD-11.</li><li>Organic mental disorders were F00–F09.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 uses ‘intellectual disability’; DSM-5-TR uses ‘intellectual developmental disorder’.</li><li>ICD-11 also has ‘provisional’ disorder of intellectual development for young children.</li><li>MHCA 2017 still uses the phrase ‘mental retardation’ in its definition.</li><li>A day-care centre teaches job skills and daily-living skills to people with chronic schizophrenia to reduce disability and prevent relapse. In Caplan’s model this is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — RML 2022, M.Phil Entrance 2021, IMHANS 2021. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 88, "question": "A day-care centre teaches job skills and daily-living skills to people with chronic schizophrenia to reduce disability and prevent relapse. In Caplan’s model this is:", "options": ["Health promotion", "Primary prevention", "Secondary prevention", "Tertiary prevention"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Gerald Caplan (1964): primary prevention lowers the number of new cases; secondary prevention shortens illness through early detection and treatment; tertiary prevention reduces long-term disability and relapse through rehabilitation.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Secondary prevention = early detection and prompt treatment.</li><li>Primary prevention = stopping new cases from occurring.</li><li>Health promotion improves general well-being in everyone.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Caplan is also known for crisis intervention theory.</li><li>Community mental health focuses heavily on primary and secondary prevention.</li><li>Psychiatric rehabilitation is the core of tertiary prevention.</li><li>A 25-year-old reports inattention and restlessness since about age 10. Under DSM-5, can ADHD be diagnosed?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — GMCH 2023, JEMAS 2022, M.Phil Entrance 2021. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 89, "question": "A 25-year-old reports inattention and restlessness since about age 10. Under DSM-5, can ADHD be diagnosed?", "options": ["Yes — several symptoms must be present before age 12", "Yes — but only if symptoms began after age 18", "No — symptoms must begin before age 7", "No — ADHD cannot be diagnosed in adults"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 raised the age-of-onset rule from 7 to 12 years (several symptoms present before 12) and allows diagnosis in adults, who need 5 rather than 6 symptoms in a domain.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>‘Before 7’ was the DSM-IV rule.</li><li>DSM-5 explicitly allows adult ADHD.</li><li>Onset after 18 would not fit ADHD.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5 also allows ADHD to be diagnosed together with autism.</li><li>Presentations: predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, combined.</li><li>ICD-11 code for ADHD: 6A05.</li><li>After being bitten, a boy becomes afraid of all dogs, and each time he avoids a dog his fear eases. Which perspective best explains how this fear started and is kept going?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 90, "question": "After being bitten, a boy becomes afraid of all dogs, and each time he avoids a dog his fear eases. Which perspective best explains how this fear started and is kept going?", "options": ["Humanistic (blocked self-actualisation)", "Psychodynamic (unconscious conflict)", "Behavioural (conditioning and reinforcement)", "Biological (neurotransmitter imbalance)"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Clinical psychology foundations", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The behavioural perspective (Pavlov, Watson, Skinner) sees abnormal behaviour as learnt through classical and operant conditioning and modelling — so it can be unlearnt.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Psychodynamic – unconscious conflicts from early childhood.</li><li>Humanistic – blocked growth and self-actualisation.</li><li>Biological – genes, brain structure and neurochemistry.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>J&amp;K 2023 asked which perspective links childhood trauma to OCD (psychodynamic).</li><li>The cognitive perspective focuses on faulty thinking.</li><li>Modern clinical practice integrates these in the biopsychosocial model.</li><li>All of the following are components of the Health Belief Model, EXCEPT:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — GBU 2025, NFSU 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 91, "question": "All of the following are components of the Health Belief Model, EXCEPT:", "options": ["Perceived benefits", "Subjective norms", "Perceived susceptibility", "Cues to action"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Health Belief Model includes perceived susceptibility, perceived severity, perceived benefits, perceived barriers, cues to action and (later) self-efficacy. Subjective norms belong to the Theory of Planned Behaviour.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Perceived susceptibility is a core HBM component.</li><li>Perceived benefits is a core HBM component.</li><li>Cues to action (e.g. a reminder) is an HBM component.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>The HBM was developed by US Public Health Service researchers in the 1950s (Rosenstock and others).</li><li>Ajzen’s Theory of Planned Behaviour adds perceived behavioural control.</li><li>Prochaska’s stages of change is another health behaviour model.</li><li>In ICD-10, F90 refers to:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>1 paper</b> — J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 92, "question": "In ICD-10, F90 refers to:", "options": ["Tic disorders", "Conduct disorders", "Emotional disorders with onset specific to childhood", "Hyperkinetic disorders"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> F90 is hyperkinetic disorders (F90.0 disturbance of activity and attention — the ICD-10 equivalent of ADHD; F90.1 hyperkinetic conduct disorder).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Conduct disorders – F91.</li><li>Tic disorders – F95.</li><li>Childhood emotional disorders – F93.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>F90–F98: behavioural and emotional disorders with onset usually in childhood and adolescence.</li><li>F98.0 nonorganic enuresis; F98.1 nonorganic encopresis.</li><li>ICD-11 ADHD code: 6A05.</li><li>Paul Meehl’s Clinical versus Statistical Prediction (1954) concluded that:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, J&amp;K 2023. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 93, "question": "Paul Meehl’s Clinical versus Statistical Prediction (1954) concluded that:", "options": ["Prediction in psychology is impossible", "Projective tests predicted best", "Clinical intuition was clearly better than formulas", "Actuarial (statistical) prediction was as good as or better than clinical judgement"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Reviewing many studies, Meehl found that mechanical, actuarial combination of data predicted outcomes at least as well as, and often better than, clinicians’ judgements.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>This is the opposite of his conclusion.</li><li>Projective tests were not shown to predict best.</li><li>He did not say prediction is impossible.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Later meta-analyses (Grove et al., 2000) supported Meehl.</li><li>Meehl also wrote on base rates with Albert Rosen (1955).</li><li>Clinical judgement remains important for hypotheses and context.</li><li>A child with autism spectrum disorder needs ‘very substantial support’ for social communication. Under DSM-5, his severity for this domain is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, this exact point hasn't appeared directly, but it sits right next to frequently asked topics — we recommend a quick revision."}, {"id": 94, "question": "A child with autism spectrum disorder needs ‘very substantial support’ for social communication. Under DSM-5, his severity for this domain is:", "options": ["Level 2", "Level 1", "Level 3", "Severe intellectual impairment"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> DSM-5 rates ASD severity by the support needed, separately for social communication and for restricted, repetitive behaviours: Level 1 ‘requiring support’, Level 2 ‘requiring substantial support’, Level 3 ‘requiring very substantial support’.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Level 2 = ‘requiring substantial support’.</li><li>Level 1 = ‘requiring support’.</li><li>Intellectual impairment is a separate specifier, not the ASD severity level.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Specifiers: with or without intellectual impairment; with or without language impairment.</li><li>Intellectual developmental disorder severity is based on adaptive functioning.</li><li>ICD-11 ASD (6A02) uses intellectual and language specifiers.</li><li>The Social Readjustment Rating Scale of Holmes and Rahe focuses upon:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>3 papers</b> — IHBAS 2025, RINPAS 2024, RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 95, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · IHBAS 2025</span>The Social Readjustment Rating Scale of Holmes and Rahe focuses upon:", "options": ["Life events", "Physiological reactions", "Cognitive changes", "Daily hassles"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Health psychology & stress", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> The Social Readjustment Rating Scale (1967) gives ‘life change units’ to major life events (death of spouse = 100, divorce = 73 and so on); high totals predict illness.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Physiological reactions are not measured by it.</li><li>Cognitive changes are not its focus.</li><li>Daily hassles were measured by Kanner and Lazarus’s Hassles Scale.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Positive events such as marriage also count as stressful.</li><li>A total above about 300 points was linked with illness risk.</li><li>Critics note it ignores how the person appraises events.</li><li>Asked what ‘a rolling stone gathers no moss’ means, a patient replies, ‘Stones roll, so moss cannot grow on them.’ This mainly shows:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 96, "question": "Asked what ‘a rolling stone gathers no moss’ means, a patient replies, ‘Stones roll, so moss cannot grow on them.’ This mainly shows:", "options": ["Poor insight into illness", "Impaired abstract thinking (concrete thinking)", "Impaired attention and concentration", "Impaired recent memory"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Proverb interpretation tests abstraction. A literal answer shows concrete thinking, seen in schizophrenia, organic brain disease and low intelligence.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Attention is tested with serial 7s or digit span.</li><li>Insight is about awareness of illness.</li><li>Recent memory is tested with recall of objects after a few minutes.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Similarities (‘How are an orange and a banana alike?’) also test abstraction.</li><li>Cultural familiarity with the proverb must be checked.</li><li>Abstraction is part of the cognitive section of the MSE.</li><li>A woman’s depressive syndrome is judged to be a direct result of untreated hypothyroidism. In ICD-11 this is classified as a:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 97, "question": "A woman’s depressive syndrome is judged to be a direct result of untreated hypothyroidism. In ICD-11 this is classified as a:", "options": ["Single episode depressive disorder", "Bodily distress disorder", "Secondary mood syndrome", "Adjustment disorder"], "correctAnswer": 2, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> This ICD-11 grouping (6E6–) covers psychotic, mood, anxiety and other syndromes caused directly by a health condition not classified as a mental disorder — replacing much of ICD-10’s ‘organic’ category (F06).</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>A primary depressive disorder is not used when a medical condition directly causes the syndrome.</li><li>Adjustment disorder follows a stressful life event, not a hormone problem.</li><li>Bodily distress disorder is about distressing bodily symptoms.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Example: secondary mood syndrome due to hypothyroidism.</li><li>DSM-5 uses ‘due to another medical condition’ for the same idea.</li><li>Substance-induced syndromes are coded under disorders due to substance use.</li><li>The Draw-a-Person test as a projective measure of personality was developed by:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 98, "question": "The Draw-a-Person test as a projective measure of personality was developed by:", "options": ["Florence Goodenough", "Karen Machover", "Saul Rosenzweig", "John Buck"], "correctAnswer": 1, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Karen Machover (1949) used human figure drawings as a projective test of personality. Florence Goodenough (1926) had earlier used the Draw-a-Man test to estimate children’s intelligence.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Goodenough used drawings to measure intelligence, not personality.</li><li>John Buck developed the House-Tree-Person test.</li><li>Rosenzweig developed the Picture-Frustration study.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Harris revised Goodenough’s test (Goodenough-Harris, 1963).</li><li>Projective drawings are quick but have weak validity.</li><li>Kinetic Family Drawing asks the child to draw the family doing something.</li><li>Which of the following is a proposed criteria set in the current DSM that involves cutting, burning or otherwise intentionally hurting one’s own body without the intent to kill oneself?</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — RML 2022, IMHANS 2021. We recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 99, "question": "<span class=\"pyq-badge\">PYQ · RML 2022</span>Which of the following is a proposed criteria set in the current DSM that involves cutting, burning or otherwise intentionally hurting one’s own body without the intent to kill oneself?", "options": ["Internet gaming disorder", "Attenuated psychosis syndrome", "Persistent complex bereavement", "Non-suicidal self-injury"], "correctAnswer": 3, "subject": "Psychopathology & classification", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> Non-suicidal self-injury (NSSI) is a condition for further study in Section III of DSM-5: deliberate self-inflicted damage to the body surface on 5 or more days in the past year, without suicidal intent.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>Internet gaming disorder is also in Section III, but it is about excessive gaming.</li><li>Attenuated psychosis syndrome is about mild psychotic-like symptoms.</li><li>Persistent complex bereavement became prolonged grief disorder in DSM-5-TR.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>DSM-5-TR added ICD-10-CM codes for NSSI and suicidal behaviour.</li><li>Suicidal behaviour disorder is the related Section III condition.</li><li>NSSI is common in borderline personality disorder but not limited to it.</li><li>On the Vineland Social Maturity Scale, an 8-year-old girl obtains a social age of 6 years. Her social quotient (SQ) is:</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, we recommend you keep this area ready."}, {"id": 100, "question": "On the Vineland Social Maturity Scale, an 8-year-old girl obtains a social age of 6 years. Her social quotient (SQ) is:", "options": ["75", "133", "48", "86"], "correctAnswer": 0, "subject": "Clinical assessment in practice", "subtopic": "", "difficulty": "", "explanation": "<p class=\"ex-why\"><b>Why:</b> SQ = (Social age ÷ Chronological age) × 100 = (6 ÷ 8) × 100 = 75.</p><div class=\"ex-sub\">Why not the others</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>133 = 8 ÷ 6 × 100 (ratio reversed).</li><li>48 = 6 × 8 (multiplied, not divided).</li><li>86 is not obtained from these figures.</li></ul><div class=\"ex-sub\">📌 Related topics you should know</div><ul class=\"ex-list\"><li>RML 2022 asked what the VSMS measures and who devised it (Edgar Doll).</li><li>The Indian adaptation was by A.J. Malin.</li><li>SQ is used with IQ in assessing intellectual disability.</li><li>— End of Mock 9 —</li><li>Next: Mock 10 (16 October) — Neuropsychology &amp; Ethics (APA &amp; Indian).  Queries: team@psychlogic.in</li></ul>", "examWatch": "As per PsychLogic's analysis of 22 previous-year papers, related questions have appeared in <b>2 papers</b> — JEMAS 2024, RML 2022. We recommend you keep this area ready."}];
